  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert $A\\cos(\\omega t + \\phi)$ into $\\operatorname{Re}\\{Ae^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}\\}$ and its equivalent conjugate complex-exponential sum.",
    "section_count": 6,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A cosine like $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$ can be rewritten so the time variation stays in $e^{j\\omega t}$ and the amplitude-phase information stays in a fixed complex factor. That fixed factor is the phasor $Ae^{j\\phi}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j\\omega t}=\\cos(\\omega t)+j\\sin(\\omega t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Taking the real part of that identity gives the base case $\\cos(\\omega t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j\\omega t}\\}$. So the unit cosine is already the real part of a rotating complex exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}\\{Ae^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}\\}=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If we multiply by $A$ and place the phase $\\phi$ in the phasor factor, the same rule becomes $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{Ae^{j\\phi}e^{j\\omega t}\\}$. The amplitude and phase stay fixed; only $e^{j\\omega t}$ carries time.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the form to recognize in later steps: the cosine is read from a real-part expression, not rebuilt from scratch. The same idea can also be written as a mirrored conjugate-exponential pair, which the next examples will unpack.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Keep the two roles separate: $Ae^{j\\phi}$ is the fixed phasor, and $e^{j\\omega t}$ is the time-varying factor. When you see $A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, the conversion target is the real part of that product.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Writing a Unit Cosine as a Real Part",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $\\cos(4t)$ as a real part.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a unit cosine with no phase shift, so the phasor factor should be just $1$. That means the target form is the real part of the matching complex exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j4t}=\\cos(4t)+j\\sin(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Taking the real part of both sides gives $\\cos(4t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j4t}\\}$. This is the zero-phase base case, and later examples will keep the same real-part structure while changing only amplitude or phase.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Including Amplitude in the Real-Part Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $5\\cos(4t)$ as a real part with the amplitude inside the phasor factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the unit-cosine identity from the previous example. Multiplying by $5$ keeps the time factor $e^{j4t}$ unchanged and moves the amplitude into the phasor factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "5\\cos(4t)=5\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j4t}\\}=\\operatorname{Re}\\{5e^{j4t}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the canonical real-part form is $5\\cos(4t)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{5e^{j4t}\\}$. The only change from the unit-cosine case is the amplitude inside the phasor factor; the oscillation in $e^{j4t}$ stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Including Phase in the Complex Phasor Factor",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $4\\cos(3t+\\pi/6)$ as a real part with the phase isolated in the phasor factor.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Identify the parts first: $A=4$, $\\omega=3$, and $\\phi=\\pi/6$. The phase belongs in the fixed phasor factor $4e^{j\\pi/6}$, while $e^{j3t}$ remains the time factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4\\cos(3t+\\pi/6)=4\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j(3t+\\pi/6)}\\}=4\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j3t}\\}=\\operatorname{Re}\\{4e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j3t}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the canonical form is $4\\cos(3t+\\pi/6)=\\operatorname{Re}\\{4e^{j\\pi/6}e^{j3t}\\}$. The new ingredient is the phase factor inside the phasor; the time dependence stays in $e^{j3t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Writing a Cosine as a Conjugate Exponential Sum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Rewrite $2\\cos(5t)$ as a sum of conjugate complex exponentials.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From Euler's formula, a cosine is the average of a complex exponential and its conjugate. For $2\\cos(5t)$, the amplitude splits evenly, so each term gets a coefficient of $1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\cos(5t)=2\\cdot\\frac{e^{j5t}+e^{-j5t}}{2}=e^{j5t}+e^{-j5t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the conjugate-sum form is $2\\cos(5t)=e^{j5t}+e^{-j5t}$. The key pattern is symmetry: opposite exponential signs and equal half-amplitude coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Representing Cosines with Complex Exponentials",
        "section_index": 6,
        "section_title": "Converting Between Real-Part Form and Conjugate-Sum Form",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert $\\frac{5}{2}e^{j(4t-\\pi/6)}+\\frac{5}{2}e^{-j(4t-\\pi/6)}$ into a real-part form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a conjugate pair with equal coefficients, so it should collapse to a single cosine. Here the pair has amplitude $5$ and phase $-\\pi/6$, with $e^{j4t}$ carrying the time dependence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\frac{5}{2}e^{j(4t-\\pi/6)}+\\frac{5}{2}e^{-j(4t-\\pi/6)}=5\\,\\operatorname{Re}\\{e^{j(4t-\\pi/6)}\\}=\\operatorname{Re}\\{5e^{-j\\pi/6}e^{j4t}\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the canonical real-part form is $\\operatorname{Re}\\{5e^{-j\\pi/6}e^{j4t}\\}$. The phase sits in the phasor factor, and the time factor stays $e^{j4t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\operatorname{Re}\\{5e^{-j\\pi/6}e^{j4t}\\}=5\\cos(4t-\\pi/6)=\\frac{5}{2}e^{j(4t-\\pi/6)}+\\frac{5}{2}e^{-j(4t-\\pi/6)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
